## The Numbers Are In

So far, 2026 has witnessed a staggering 337 recalls. Of these, 91 were Class I--posing the highest health risk. The lead offenders? Familiar faces like Moonlight Packing Corporation and Albertsons Companies LLC, both tied with 11 recalls each this year. Perhaps consistency isn't always a virtue.

## This Week's Showcase

### Listeria: Back in the Spotlight
Hemu Trading Inc tops our recall roster with a Class I notice on their enoki mushrooms, tainted with Listeria. A stark reminder of the pathogen's persistence in refrigerated produce.

### Undeclared Allergens: A Sneaky Risk
- Schreiber Foods Inc mixed up their cream cheese products, resulting in almond exposure for unsuspecting customers.
- Costco's iconic madeleines were found harboring undeclared hazelnut and soy lecithin.

### Color Me Disapproved
Guangdong Zonegoing Food Co., LTD managed to scatter unapproved food dyes (Yellow 5, Yellow 6, Allura Red) across multiple flavors of their 52USA Popping Boba.

### Foreign Objects: A Crunch You Didn't Expect
Lactalis Canada Inc's creme brulee might come with an unwelcome glass topping, proving that some surprises aren't so sweet.

## Repeat Spell for Repeat Offenders
CHETAK NEW YORK LLC continues its dubious reign with 52 recalls, all Class I. If there were a hall of shame, they'd be a lifetime member.

## Monthly Recap
With 84 recalls last month, the pace doesn't seem to be slowing. May has already brought us 10 new cases, and we're only halfway through.

## What We Learned
The data reflects a systemic issue with food safety compliance. Cost-cutting measures shouldn't jeopardize public health. Brands must prioritize stringent quality checks, and consumers should stay informed.

**Stay sharp, stay safe.**
